Nowak & Pion, 2014). In accordance with the multiple resources model of Wickens (2002) tasks using the visual channel to process spatial information and including manual reactions interfere most with the driving tasks as this also requires these resources. The secondary task and the driving task compete for these resources. As these are limited, performance decrements result. Looking at the interference due to texting while driving a bit more closely, information from the environment relevant for driving is simply not available to drivers looking at their smartphone. Thus, they fail to notice that drivers in front are braking (rear-end crashes, see Victor et al., 2014) or that they are leaving the lane (leading to single vehicle crashes). Thus, at least for texting on the mobile phone it should be obvious for every driver that this is detrimental for driving. However, observational studies have found large percentages of drivers texting when driving (e.g. Asgharabad, Tahami & Khanjani, 2013; Pickrell and Li, 2016; Vollrath et al., 2016). One reason for this may be that while drivers are aware that visual distraction is dangerous in general, they underestimate the actual risk for themselves (Huemer & Vollrath, 2011). In addition, to make texting while driving seem safer, drivers may also adapt their behavior by only engaging in secondary tasks in relatively low risk driving situations (e.g. Huth, Sanchez & Brusque, 2015; Metz, Landau & Hargutt, 2015) or by driving more slowly and with a longer headway (e.g., Fitch, Grove, Hanowski & Perez, 2014; Oviedo-Trespalacios, Haque, King & Washington, 2016). This idea of compensation is also in line with the multiple resources theory. While resources are limited they may also be shared. If both tasks require only a small part of the available resources, as in texting while driving on an easy stretch of the road, it may be possible that both tasks can be done concurrently without interference. Thus, combining easy driving situations with easy secondary tasks may work well even when both use the same resources. This point has also been made by Shiffrin and Schneider (1977) in their theory of automatic and controlled human information processing. If tasks are very well learned they can be performed automatically and do not require many processing capacities. This idea was also extended in a review by Engström et al. (2017) for secondary tasks which primarily increase cognitive, but not visual load. In their review they found that cognitive load only impairs subtasks of driving that require cognitive control, but not subtasks with automatic performance. Finally, in their integrative review of dual-task and task-switching research, Koch, Poljac, Müller and Kiesel (2018) found three processes which could lead to practice effects in dual-task performance one of which was task automatization. If this idea is transferred to the effect of secondary tasks while driving, a new moderating factor is introduced for both the driving task and secondary tasks. With regard to driving, one would expect that driving becomes more and more automatic the longer and more one drives. Drivers with a longer driving experience and drivers with a larger mileage should mainly use automatic information processing while driving. For texting on the mobile phone, similar effects would be expected. People who text all the time and have done that for several years should also mainly use automatic information processing while texting. Thus, experienced drivers and competent mobile phone users should be best able to text while driving without losses in either the primary or secondary task. A study by Rumschlag et al. (2015) compared skilled and not skilled texters in a driving simulation and indeed found differences: For the not skilled texters, lane excursions were the larger the longer the text messages were. Skilled texters did not show this effect (perhaps because their text messages were short and with less variance), but a strong age effect occurred. The older the drivers were, the more lane excursions occurred. One possible reason for this effect could be the decrease of resources with age which then leads to an increase of impairment. However, driving experience was not examined in this study. Thus, the combined influence of driving experience on the one hand and texting experience on the other has still not been examined to our knowledge. In addition, it would also be interesting to examine the effect of specific experience of texting while driving, as that may be a skill in itself. However, as texting while driving is forbidden in Germany we assumed that it would be difficult to obtain true and realistic answers to the question of how often one texts while driving. In order to avoid this bias we decided to focus on experience with texting and with driving, separately. This idea was tested in a driving simulator study using the lane change task (Mattes, 2003; ISO/DIS 26022, 2010) as a simple driving simulator and comparing high and low experienced drivers and high and low competent mobile phone users while driving only and when texting while driving. Additionally, it was examined to what extent drivers can assess their performance decrements due to texting while driving. As described above, our hypotheses were that experienced drivers and experienced texters would both show better dual-task performance (driving and texting) than inexperienced drivers or texters. Both effects should also add up so that experienced drivers who were also experienced texters should have the least negative effects of texting while driving. The method is described in the next section.
2. Method
2.1. Experimental design
The basic aim of the study was to examine the effect of practice in driving and texting on the phone on the ability to combine both tasks and to, especially, drive without losses in driving performance. To this aim, the study used a 2 2 quasi-experimental design comparing the two independent variables ‘driving experience’ (low vs. high) and ‘texting competence’ (low vs. high). A quasi-experiment was used as the two independent variables could not be actively varied. From this design, four independent groups resulted which are shown in Table 1.
