For the driving experience, this was included in the selection of the subjects. For drivers with a low driving experience we looked for subjects driving a maximum of 5000 km per year and having their driver’s licence for a maximum of three years. For the highly experienced group, drivers were supposed to drive at least 10,000 km per year and have their driver’s licence for at least five years. 20 drivers were selected for each group. To select for texting competency, a simple test was done using the personal mobile phone of the subject. Every driver was told to type a given text (79 words). They were supposed to do this as fast as possible and with so few errors as possible. The time required and the number of errors were measured. When looking at the distribution of time and errors it was obvious that subjects used different strategies. Some typed very rapidly, but had many errors. Others took their time, but had hardly any errors. Thus, for the selection of texting competency, a combined measure of errors and time was used. Subjects took from 98 to 492 s to complete the text and did 0–16 errors. For the highly competent texting group, subjects were allowed up to 192 s to type the text while doing less than seven typing mistakes. Thus, in the highly competent group the mean time was 147 s and 2.1 errors. The less competent group took 261 s and had 3.5 errors. As shown in Table 1, this resulted in 20 subjects in each group. However, there were more highly competent texters in the low driving experience group and less in the high driving experience groups. In an experiment, the four groups would have been randomly assigned thus controlling for other confounding factors. As this was not possible in this quasi-experiment, we looked at possible confounders, the most important being age. Fig. 1 shows the mean age in the four groups. For drivers with a low driving experience, the two groups of differing texting competency were of the same age. However, in the group with high driving experience, the age of the drivers with a low texting competency was 42 years as compared to 26 years for the drivers with a high texting competency. Thus, if a main effect of texting competency was found in the group with high driving experience, this could also be due to the older age of the less texting competent drivers. In order to measure the effect of texting on driving, the lane change task (LCT; Mattes, 2003) was used. This is a simple driving simulation which runs on a desktop computer using a gaming steering wheel and pedals to steer a simulated car. The main driving task is to drive on a three-lane road. About every 150 m a sign becomes visible which indicates the current target lane. The driver is instructed to change the lane as soon and fast as possible when the sign becomes visible. The driver is instructed to drive with a constant speed of 60 km/h. Driving faster is prevented by the simulation. Thus, drivers are instructed to just go as fast as possible. One trial consists of 18 lane changes with a randomized order of these lane changes. The recommended measure of driving performance is the mean of the deviation from an ideal (artificial) trajectory. This assumes the driver to stay at the middle of the lane and then make a lane change at 30 m before the lane change sign, taking 10 m to change the lane. This measure was also used in the present study. Additionally, the driving task in the lane change task basically comprises lane changes (in reaction to respective signs) in alternation with lane keeping. Drivers might try to synchronize their texting with the driving task by focusing on a lane change and then do the texting in the meantime until the next lane change was required. In order to examine that, the mean deviation was also computed for lane change and lane
