keeping parts of the task, separately. The lane change phase was set to begin when the sign became visible (40 m away from the sign) until 60 m after the sign. The next 40 m were then regarded as lane keeping phase. This task was first learned by all subjects by doing 4 practice trials. After that, every subject drove two trials with and two further trials without texting. The order of these four trials was randomized. The results from the two trials without and with texting were averaged to obtain more stable parameters. Thus, ‘texting’ was introduced as an additional independent variable (with texting vs. driving only). With regard to the texting task, the experimentor wrote a text message every 20 s to the subjects containg simple questions like ‘‘What kind of weather is today?”, ‘‘Which was the last book you have read?”, ‘‘What is your favourite drink”, etc. A complete list of the 18 questions used can be obtained from the authors on request. Besides the driving parameter ‘mean deviation from ideal lane’, every driver was asked to evaluate the own performance after each trial. School grades were used ranging from 1 (very good) to 6 (very bad) in order to examine how well drivers can assess their performance.
2.2. Participants
Table 1 shows the distribution of the 40 drivers in the four groups. 10 of them were male and 30 females. The mean age was 28 years (sd = 12.6). Overall, they had their driver’s licence for a mean of 120 months (sd = 143) and drove on average between 5000 and 10 000 km per year.
2.3. Procedure
After arrival, subjects were instructed that the study was concerned with examining the effects of using a mobile phone to type messages while driving in a simple driving simulator. They were also told that we were interested in the effects of practice with typing and did a simple typing test. The results were used as assessment of typing competence (see above). After obtaining sociodemographic data subjects were instructed in the lane change task. They did four trials of this task in order to achieve a well-trained performance level. After that, the experimental trials began. In the two trials without distraction they were told to do the lane change task as well as they could. In the other two trials they were instructed to answer text messages where the examiner asked simple questions about daily events (e.g., ‘‘Did you drive with a bus today?”) which could be answered with a short text message. Drivers were instructed to answer as many of these questions as they could. However, driving the lane change task was still instructed to be the main task which should not suffer by the texting. After finishing, some questions concerning phone use while driving where given.
3. Results
A 2 2 2 mixed ANOVA was computed for the mean deviation from the ideal lane with ‘texting’ as within-subjects factor and ‘texting competence’ and ‘driving experience’ as between subject factors. The same ANOVA was also used for the lane keeping phase and the part of the lane changes. Table 2 gives the results. For the overall mean deviation, there was a strong main effect of texting, a second main effect of texting competence and an interaction between texting and texting competence. Additionally, there was a tendency (p = 0.054) for an interaction between driving experience and texting competence. Similar effects were found for the lane keeping phase with the exception of the interaction, which was not significant. In contrast, the main effect of texting competence was not as strong while changing the lane, but the interaction of driving experience and texting competence was clearly significant while changing the lane. As the weak interaction between driving experience and texting competence in the overall mean deviation from the ideal lane seems to be due to the lane change phase (significant interaction there), Fig. 2 gives the mean deviation while changing the lane. It shows that only drivers with a high driving competence and a high texting competence perform better than drivers with a high driving competence but with a low texting competence. Thus, texting competence has a positive effect on
