4. Discussion
In accordance to the theory of Shiffrin & Schneider (1977) well-trained phone users who can text fast and with few errors are not impaired as much by texting while driving as compared to not-so-competent phone users. Their handling the phone is probably mainly done by automatic information processing, thus requiring less resources which does not interfere with driving as much as for less competent phone users. From a behavioural point of view, they may simply type faster and are thus distracted from driving for shorter periods of time. As we were not able to record the time spent typing this interpretation could not be tested in the present study. However, although the deviation from an ideal lane position during texting was less than for the less competent phone users, there was still a significant deterioration as compared to driving without distraction. As the driving simulation uses a very limited driving situation, it is hard to predict the effects of phone use competency for crash risk. However, the lane change task is a well-established tool for assessing safety-relevant driving behaviour. Moreover, when the effects are significant in such a relatively easy driving task they could be even stronger in real driving in complex and demanding situations. However, it could also be that in real driving compensatory behaviour (only texting on easy parts of the route) is more likely than in the driving simulator study. Thus, replicating this effect in real driving or at least in more complex driving situations in the driving simulator would be very interesting. As there was some confounding between age and texting competence, it would also be fine to replicate this study with a more balanced design. Additionally, it would be very interesting to also use a group with a high texting competency while driving. This would require to find drivers who are willing to confirm that they often text while driving all though this is forbidden in Germany. However, comparing this to texting competency per se one could examine whether texting while a driving as a dual task is a competency per se. This would contribute to the discussion in dual task research whether dual task performance is a special competency which can be trained. In order to avoid the difficulty of finding drivers willing to confirm that they are texting while driving one could also use naturalistic driving data to select similar drivers (e.g., with regard to sex, age, driving experience, annual milage) who either text frequently while driving and who do it only infrequently. One would then have to select similar driving situations where both groups of drivers are texting and obtain some measure of driving performance, e.g. standard deviation of lane position as an indicator of lane keeping performance. While this would be a very realistic and valid approach, it could be difficult to control for all confounding factors like traffic situation, traffic density, environment etc. With regard to driving experience, one would also expect a smaller effect of texting for experienced drivers. However, this was not the case. One reason could be that experience in real driving does not transfer to driving in the driving simulator. However, at least for drivers with a high texting competency (younger drivers) those with a larger driving experience performed much better in the driving simulator than those with a smaller driving experience. This result supports the validity of the simulation. However, this did not affect the impairment due to texting while driving which was similar regardless of driving experience. From this viewpoint it does not make sense to have stronger regulations for younger drivers or to be more lax for experienced drivers. Or, to put it differently, the problem is more strongly related to the secondary task, not so much to the primary task of driving. It could also be that the driving task was just too easy so that even less experienced drivers were easily able to perform well (floor effect). Thus, it would be interesting to see if an effect of driving experience was found in complex traffic situations with a more difficult driving task which should present few problems for the experienced drivers but require a large amount of processing resources for less experienced drivers.
