When asking the drivers directly after their trip about the quality of their driving performance all drivers were well aware of the loss of driving performance due to texting on the mobile phone. This may partially be due to an answer bias of social desirability knowing that one should not text on the phone while driving. It was also interesting to see that highly competent phone users rated themselves somewhat better than not-so-competent users. However, the objectively smaller detrimental effect of texting in highly competent users was not found in the subjective assessment. This could be due to the answer bias mentioned. However, it could also mean that the self-assessment is not very precise and differentiated. Overall, these results show that the subjective belief that texting on the phone decreases driving performance is not sufficient to refrain drivers from doing so. This was also supported by an exploratory interview after the experiment where about 80% of the subjects indicated that they used the phone while driving at least from time to time. Why do people do this although they know that they drive less safely? One reason could be that they think that the impairment is still within a range of safe behaviour. When texting while driving, one may react more slowly and one may swerve, but drivers do not experience a crash every time they use the phone. While this is simply due to our traffic being so safe and the absolute crash risk being so small, subjectively every phone use without a crash may be experienced as a reinforcement of the impression that one is still on the safe side. Additionally, the benefits and direct positive reinforcement from using the phone may be very large as compared to the risky, but highly improbable negative results of distraction. Thus, for prevention, simply demonstrating the negative effects of mobile phone use on driving performance does not seem to be sufficient to substantially influence mobile phone users’ dangerous habits. However, the study shows that even experience texters show a significant negative effect of texting on driving performance. Presenting these (and similar) results to drivers in the context of prevention might help to clarify that experience and caution is not always sufficient to counteract these negative effects of texting. Additionally, one could easily demonstrate these negative effects to the experienced texters and thus perhaps lower their impression of being able to counteract these negative effects. However, as their seems to be a large need to interact by phone, perhaps the only solution is to further encourage the use of text-to-speech letting the phone read out the text messages and to use voice messages for the reply. This enables the driver to look at the road while being engaged in this kind of interaction. To this aim, apps could introduce a driving mode which could also be automatically be activated when the app detects that the user is driving. With regard to the limitations of the study, besides using a simple driving simulator (see discussion above) the sample was limited to a mostly young, student, female population. This was mainly due to practical availability of subjects. However, mobile phone use and a high competence in using it is more frequent in young people. Moreover, negative effects may be more pronounced in inexperienced, young drivers with their higher crash risk. However, especially with regard to driving experience, it would be interesting to replicate this study with an older sample of drivers. However, it may be difficult to find older drivers with a comparable high texting competence as younger drivers.
